# Balam Rush Heliux Pro HEX75 CPU cooler display controller.
#
# usb.ids mislabels this device as an "MSR-101U magnetic card reader"; it is a
# no-name microcontroller exposing a vendor-defined 64-byte HID pipe. See
# a cloned ID also used by USB relay boards.
#
# Without this, its /dev/hidraw node is root-only (crw------- root root) and
# mimarchy cannot write display frames.
#
# Access is granted the way logind grants it for the AURA controller and the
# I2C buses: a `uaccess` tag, which puts an ACL for the active seat's user on
# the node — no group, no world-writable device, and it follows logins. The
# catch is ordering. The tag is *acted on* by 73-seat-late.rules, which runs
# the `uaccess` builtin for every device already tagged when it is evaluated;
# a rule in 99- adds the tag after that has happened, so the tag was set and
# nothing ever looked at it. This rule therefore runs the builtin itself.
# (An earlier version of this comment blamed `udevadm trigger` for not
# applying the ACL; the trigger was fine, the ordering was the problem.)
#
# GROUP="input" stays as a fallback for a machine where logind is not
# managing the seat, but it is no longer what makes this work: Omarchy 4's
# migration of 2026-09-03 removes users from `input` — membership is raw
# access to every keyboard — so on Omarchy the group grants nothing.
#
# install.sh does not have root copy this file out of the checkout — it prints
# a command with the rule spelled out inline, so what root writes is what the
# user just read, not what a user-writable directory holds by the time sudo
# runs. This copy documents the rule and anchors the sync check in
# tests/test_install_inputs.py; the rule is one line so the two can be
# compared byte for byte.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="5131", ATTRS{idProduct}=="2007", TAG+="uaccess", RUN{builtin}+="uaccess", GROUP="input", MODE="0660"
